Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.2.10", "inferred_role": "tier0_domain_controller", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:18:42+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-18T23:22:18+0100", "agent_name": "DC-CORP-01"},
    {"ip": "10.30.7.84", "inferred_role": "non_pam_enrolled_workstation_source", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:18:42+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-18T23:28:18+0100", "agent_name": "WS-IT-12"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts (Privileged)", "technique": "T1078.002", "what_happened": "At 2027-11-18 23:18:42 CET, Event 4624 'An account was successfully logged on' fired on DC-CORP-01 (10.30.2.10) with TargetUserName=domadmin01, LogonType=10 (RemoteInteractive = RDP), IpAddress=10.30.7.84, AuthenticationPackageName=Negotiate. This is an interactive RDP logon by a Tier-0 Domain Admin account directly to a Domain Controller. Per corporate Tier-0 access policy, the domadmin01 account MUST ONLY be usable via the PAM tool (CyberArk vault) which mediates the session, records keystrokes, and enforces just-in-time credential checkout. A direct 4624 from a non-PAM source workstation is a structural policy violation regardless of any subsequent action.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 on agent.name=DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=domadmin01 + LogonType=10 + IpAddress=10.30.7.84. The source IP 10.30.7.84 (WS-IT-12) is NOT enrolled in the PAM-approved jump-host allowlist — direct credential use bypassing the vault.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:18:42+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Modify Authentication Process (PAM Bypass)", "technique": "T1556", "what_happened": "At 23:18:48 CET (6 seconds after the 4624), the Wazuh PAM-integration decoder (CyberArk vault integration) executed its mandatory correlation check: 'for every Tier-0 admin 4624, find the matching PAM session checkout record within ±5 minutes'. Result: NO MATCHING PAM SESSION RECORD found for domadmin01 from source 10.30.7.84 in the [23:13:42, 23:23:42] window. This is the PAM bypass signature — the attacker used the domadmin01 credential DIRECTLY (cached / stolen / phished / replayed) without going through the vault checkout workflow, which would have (a) recorded the session, (b) mediated the connection via an approved jump host, (c) enforced MFA-on-checkout, (d) issued a time-bound one-time credential.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom decoder rule (rule.id 100912, level 14, group=pam_bypass): correlates Windows Security 4624 events with TargetUserName ∈ Tier-0-admin-list against CyberArk session-checkout API stream. Missing-checkout = PAM bypass alert. Univariate signature: privileged 4624 with NO paired vault record.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:18:48+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Create Account", "technique": "T1136.002", "what_happened": "At 23:22:14 CET (~3.5 minutes after the bypass), Event 4720 'A user account was created' fired on DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=svc-backup-2 and SubjectUserName=domadmin01. The attacker — operating under the bypassed Tier-0 session — created a new domain user account with a service-account-styled name designed to blend in with legitimate backup-service accounts. Rogue admin-account creation is a classic post-compromise persistence mechanism: the new account survives password resets on the originally-compromised account.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4720 on agent.name=DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=svc-backup-2 + SubjectUserName=domadmin01. Direct cause-effect: the bypassed domadmin01 session is the actor; the new account is the persistence artifact.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:22:14+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Account Manipulation", "technique": "T1098 + T1078.002", "what_happened": "At 23:22:18 CET (4 seconds after the create), Event 4732 'A member was added to a security-enabled local group' fired on DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=svc-backup-2, GroupName='Domain Admins', SubjectUserName=domadmin01. The just-created svc-backup-2 was immediately elevated to Domain Admins. This is now a HIDDEN TIER-0 ADMIN account, named to look like a backup service. If the SOC simply disables domadmin01 in response to the bypass alert, the attacker retains full Tier-0 access via svc-backup-2.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4732 on agent.name=DC-CORP-01 with TargetUserName=svc-backup-2 + GroupName=Domain Admins + SubjectUserName=domadmin01. 4-second gap from 4720 = scripted / automated post-create elevation (humans do not type the elevation command 4 seconds after the create).", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:22:18+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Governance Violation (Correlated PAM Alert)", "technique": "T1556", "what_happened": "At 23:24:42 CET, the Wazuh PAM-integration decoder fired a SECOND correlated alert: 'Tier-0 admin actions (account creation + Domain Admin group add) performed by domadmin01 on DC-CORP-01 in the time-window [23:18:42, 23:24:42] WITHOUT any preceding PAM session-checkout record'. This is the governance-violation correlation: not just the logon was unmediated, but the destructive Tier-0 operations (4720 + 4732) all happened in an unmediated session. Per Tier-0 governance policy, ANY Domain-Admin-level change MUST be preceded by a CyberArk approval-and-checkout workflow with named-individual responsibility chain.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh decoder rule (rule.id 100914, level 15, group=pam_bypass + tier0_governance): correlates Tier-0 sensitive-operation events (4720, 4732, 4728, 4756) by subjectUserName against vault checkout records. Absence of checkout AND presence of sensitive op = critical governance violation.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:24:42+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Network Architecture Violation (Non-PAM Source)", "technique": "T1556 + T1078.002", "what_happened": "At 23:28:18 CET, the Wazuh network-context decoder confirmed the source IP 10.30.7.84 maps to WS-IT-12 (IT helpdesk workstation in CMDB), and WS-IT-12 is NOT in the PAM-approved-jump-host enrollment list. PAM-approved jump hosts are a controlled set of hardened bastions (JUMP-PAM-01 through JUMP-PAM-04 per CMDB tag pam_jump_host=true) whose IPs are the ONLY sources permitted by network ACL to initiate RDP to Tier-0 hosts. The fact that a regular IT workstation could open a direct RDP to a Domain Controller proves the Tier-0 network ACL is either missing or misconfigured — the architectural control that should have made PAM bypass technically impossible is absent.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh CMDB-enrichment + network-policy-decoder cross-check: source IP 10.30.7.84 (WS-IT-12) + destination IP 10.30.2.10 (DC-CORP-01) + destination role tier0_domain_controller + source role NOT pam_jump_host → architectural violation alert. Confirms the bypass was structurally possible, not just procedurally violated.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T23:28:18+0100"}
  ],
